+## What checking can do
+
+<!-- @rice_verifier_booth -->
+<!-- @negative_control_fixture -->
+<!-- @critic_regress_stack -->
+<!-- @fluency_seam -->
+
+Four smaller works between the machine and the exit are about checking. A booth takes a question at its slot and answers it if the question is about a program’s text, and refuses it if the question is about what the program means: refused by theorem, not by a missing feature. A gauge earns its green light by first being made to go red; pull the lever that plants the fault and watch the needle swing, because a green light you have never seen fail is a claim about the instrument as much as about the world. A stack of critics, each lens pointed at what it can see, none at the dark mark on the floor: add a critic and the blind spot moves, and never goes. And a seam, where one smooth answer stands beside the same question run again, close enough that the gap cannot be talked away. Checking can catch the fault it was built to catch. It cannot certify its own ground, and none of these four claims to.
+
+## A picture of the room
+
+<!-- @print_gallery_recursion -->
+
+By the exit stands a framed picture on a low plinth. Its subject is the room it stands in, with the picture in it, and the picture in that. Follow the spiral inward and it reaches a gap where the drawing cannot close on itself. Escher left that centre blank in 1956 and put his signature in the blank; the hall keeps the gap. A construction that cannot close itself has two honest endings, hide the gap or show it, and this one shows it.
 
